\details{
For the FULL and SF versions, the output includes the 25 facet
scores followed by the 5 personality-trait domain scores. Following the APA
scoring key (Step 3), each domain score is the mean of the average scores of
its 3 primary facets (the map is stored in \code{pid_domains}). The BF version
scores its 5 domains directly from its items, and adds a \code{total} score. By
default (\code{missing = "apa"}) all versions apply the APA missing-data and
proration rule; use \code{missing = "available"} or \code{missing = "complete"} for
the traditional \code{rowMeans()} behaviors. For per-scale reliability estimates
(Cronbach's alpha, McDonald's omega), use \code{\link[=reliability_pid5]{reliability_pid5()}}.

\subsection{The PID5BF+M}{

\code{version = "BFPM"} scores the PID5BF+M of Bach et al. (2020), a 36-item
form with 6 domains: the 5 PID-5 trait domains and Anankastia. Every item
is a PID-5 item, and none is reverse-keyed. The output
is 18 facets of 2 items each, then 6 domains, in the order of the form's
key: Negative affectivity, Detachment, Antagonism, Disinhibition,
Anankastia and Psychoticism. Each domain score is the mean of its 3 facet
scores (the map is stored in \link{pid_bfpm_domains}). The 15 facets the form
shares with the PID-5 keep their PID-5 column names. The three Anankastia
facets are \code{perfectionism}, \code{rigidity} and \code{orderliness}. All six of their
items are PID-5 Rigid Perfectionism items, but these facets are not parts
of \code{rigidPerfectionism}, which this version does not score.

Scores are item means on the 0 to 3 scale, as for the other versions. The
form's published key sums the 2 items of a facet and averages the facet
sums for a domain. On complete data, the key's facet sum is
\verb{2 * pid_<facet>} and its domain score is \verb{2 * pid_<domain>}.

No missing-data rule is published for this form. Under the default
\code{missing = "apa"}, the 25\% rule applied to a 2-item facet means that any
missing item makes the facet \code{NA}, and an \code{NA} facet makes its domain
\code{NA}. So with whole-number responses \code{"apa"} gives the same output as
\code{"complete"} here. (The APA rule rounds each scale's sum, so responses
with decimals can differ.) Under \code{missing = "available"}, a facet can be
scored from one item and a domain from one or two of its facets.
}

\subsection{The PID-5 Informant Form}{

\code{version = "IRF"} scores the PID-5 Informant Form (PID-5-IRF; Markon et
al., 2013), the APA's 218-item form on which an informant rates the person
receiving care. Its output is the FULL version's: the same 25 facets and 5
domains, with the same column names and domain map (\link{pid_domains}). The
form has no counterpart to self-report items 96 and 177, so its
Anxiousness facet has 8 items and its Suspiciousness facet 6. Informant
item n is self-report item n through 95, n + 1 through 175, and n + 2
after that; \code{pid_items$IRF} holds the mapping and \code{pid_items$TextIRF} the
informant wording.

Fourteen items are reverse-scored: 7, 30, 35, 58, 87, 90, 96, 97, 130,
141, 154, 163, 208 and 213, the items the key's Facet Table marks R. The
key's Step 1 also lists items 98 and 176, which the Facet Table does not
mark and whose wording is not reversed; the package does not reverse them.

The key prints its proration step as "round up to the nearest whole
number". The package applies the nearest-whole-number rule of every other
APA PID-5 key, halves up (see \code{missing}). The choice matters only under
\code{missing = "apa"}, for a facet with 1 to 25\% of its items unanswered whose
prorated raw score has a fractional part below one half. There the
package's facet score is 1/n lower than a ceiling would give, for a facet
of n items.
}

\subsection{The PID-5-BF total score}{

\code{version = "BF"} returns a \code{total} column after its 5 domains. Markon et al.
(2024, p. 23) define it as the item-level mean over \strong{all 25 items}, not
the mean of the 5 domain means: the total "can be computed by averaging the
overall score by the total number of items in the measure (i.e., 25)". With
five equal-sized domains the two definitions coincide on complete data and
differ only when items are missing, where the published rule above governs.

The total is scored like any other scale, so \code{missing} applies to it at the
25-item level. Under \code{missing = "apa"} that means it is \code{NA} when more than
a quarter of the 25 items are unanswered (7 or more) and prorated otherwise,
independently of the domains. Because a 5-item domain is dropped at 2
unanswered items while the total tolerates 6, \strong{a total can be reported
alongside one or more \code{NA} domains} (at most 3 of the 5; blanking all five
requires 10 unanswered items, which blanks the total as well). This is the
published rule applied as written, not an oversight.

The FULL, SF, IRF and BFPM versions have no total score: the PID-5 book
defines one only for the brief form, and the PID5BF+M and PID-5-IRF
sources define none.

\strong{Errors.} With \code{append = TRUE}, a column of \code{data} whose name this call
would also produce is an error rather than an overwrite or a duplicated
column: the message names every colliding column. Re-run with
\code{append = FALSE} to return only the new columns, or drop the colliding
columns from \code{data} first. The condition is classed
\code{hitop_append_collision}, so a caller can catch this refusal by name.

An item column that cannot be read as numbers (see \code{items}) is an error
of class \code{hitop_nonnumeric_items}, raised before the collision check.
}
